title: Okta Support System
page: breaches/okta-support-har.html
as-of: 2026-11-22
promise: In 2023 a service account credential Chrome synced to an employee's personal Google profile, most likely exposed through that account or device per Okta, gave an attacker Okta's support system, whose HAR files routinely held live session tokens.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: 19 | days of access | September 28 to October 17, 2023
stat: 134 | customers had files accessed | 5 had sessions hijacked
stat: 14 | days lost to a logging gap | during Okta's own investigation
Detection came entirely from the customers. Three of them found it before Okta did: 1Password, BeyondTrust and Cloudflare.
notes:
Start with the figures the page leads with.

Between September 28 and October 17, 2023, an attacker had access to Okta's customer support case management system: 19 days. Files belonging to 134 customers were accessed, and five of them had sessions hijacked.

Okta's own investigation lost 14 days to a logging gap, which Okta later documented in detail.

The page puts one point in bold: detection came entirely from the customers. 1Password reported suspicious activity, BeyondTrust supplied the attacker IP, and Cloudflare detected and disclosed independently. The page's own count is three customers who found it before the vendor did.

Keep the two customer figures apart when you say them aloud. 134 is the number of customers whose files were accessed; five is the number whose sessions were hijacked.

== What happened, and when
layout: timeline
event: September 28, 2023 | The attacker's access to Okta's support case system begins
event: September 29 | 1Password reports suspicious activity, the day after access began
event: October 13 | BeyondTrust supplies the attacker IP that unblocks Okta's investigation
event: October 17 | The 19 days of access end
Through a compromised service account, the attacker reached customer HAR files, which routinely contain live session tokens. The page gives no date for Cloudflare's independent detection. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
The page gives these dates, and the timeline shows only those. The markers are evenly spaced, and the gaps between them are not to scale.

Between September 28 and October 17, 2023, an attacker had access to Okta's customer support case management system. 1Password reported suspicious activity on September 29, the day after access began. BeyondTrust supplied the attacker IP on October 13, and that unblocked Okta's investigation. Cloudflare detected and disclosed independently; the page gives no date for that.

Okta's investigation was blinded for fourteen days by a logging gap. The page says the gap closed only when BeyondTrust handed over the IP address on October 13. It does not say on which day the fourteen days began, so the timeline does not draw them.

The page also does not say what ended the access on October 17. If someone asks, that is the answer from the page.

== Files at 134 customers, hijacked sessions at five
layout: bars
bar: 134 | Customers whose files were accessed
bar: 5 | Customers whose sessions were hijacked
134 is under 1% of Okta's base. The page says the gap reflects token lifetime more than anything else: only tokens still valid when the attacker got to them were useful.
notes:
The bars share one scale from zero, so the short bar is drawn true to size beside the long one.

Files belonging to 134 customers were accessed, which the page puts at under 1% of Okta's base. Five customers had sessions hijacked.

The page reads the difference as a conversion rate, and says it reflects token lifetime more than anything else, since only tokens still valid when the attacker got to them were useful. Keep the words more than anything else when you say it.

Both figures count customers. The page gives no count of HAR files or of tokens, so do not turn either bar into one.

== The kill chain on one slide
layout: flow
step: 01 | Initial Access | Saved to a personal profile | T1555.003
step: 02 | Collection | HAR files with live tokens | T1213
step: 03 | Lateral Movement | Sessions hijacked at five customers | T1550.004
step: 04 | Defense Impairment | Fourteen days hidden | T1685.002
step: 05 | Detection | Customers found it | -
step: 06 | Response | Four layers of fixes | -
Arrows show the page's stage order. 1Password reported suspicious activity the day after access began, and access ran to October 17.
notes:
Here is the whole chain on one slide: the page's six stages, each with its phase and primary ATT&CK technique.

Stage 01 is how a service account credential ended up in an employee's personal Google profile. Stage 02 is the support case system, where customers' HAR files held live session tokens. Stage 03 is replaying those tokens to hijack sessions at five customers. Stage 04 is the logging gap that hid fourteen days from Okta's investigation. Stage 05 is detection, which came from customers. Stage 06 is Okta's response, across four layers.

Stages 05 and 06 map to no ATT&CK technique, so their boxes show no ID.

Read the arrows as the page's stage order, not as a strict sequence in time. By the page's own dates, 1Password reported suspicious activity on September 29, the day after access began, while access ran to October 17. The next slides take each stage in turn.

== Chrome synced a service account credential to a personal profile
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1555.003 | Credentials from Web Browsers
technique: T1078 | Valid Accounts
fact: Device | an Okta-managed laptop, not an unmanaged personal machine
fact: Action | an employee signed into a personal Google profile in Chrome
fact: Mechanism | Chrome saved and synced the credential to that profile
fact: Exposure, per Okta | most likely compromise of that personal account or device
No policy was knowingly broken: a browser feature worked as designed.
notes:
Stage 01 is initial access. The page maps it to T1555.003, Credentials from Web Browsers, and tags T1078, Valid Accounts.

Nobody did anything that felt wrong, the page says. The laptop was managed. The employee signed into their personal Google profile in Chrome, which hundreds of millions of people do daily. Chrome offered to save a password, as it always does, and synced it to the profile that was signed in, which was the personal one. From that moment a service account credential belonging to Okta lived in a consumer Google account outside Okta's control, subject to whatever security that person's personal account and devices happened to have.

Okta's own assessment is that the most likely exposure route was compromise of that personal account or device. Keep the words most likely, and keep them Okta's.

The page calls this shadow IT in miniature: the boundary was crossed not by a policy violation but by a browser feature doing exactly what it is designed to do. The next slide explains service accounts and shadow IT in general.

== What a service account is, and what shadow IT means
layout: background
source: csoh.org glossary, Service Account | https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-service-account
credit: Also from the csoh.org glossary entry Shadow IT
A service account is the non-human principal a workload uses to authenticate.

Shadow IT is unsanctioned services employees adopt outside the visibility of security and IT. The page calls stage 01 shadow IT in miniature; no policy was knowingly broken.
notes:
This is a background slide. It explains two general ideas from the csoh.org glossary.

A service account is the non-human principal a workload uses to authenticate. The glossary's examples are a GCP service account, an Azure managed identity, and a Kubernetes ServiceAccount mapped to a cloud role.

The page does not say what Okta's service account was for. It says only that Chrome saved the account's username and password into a personal Google account, and that the account could read the customer support case management system.

Shadow IT means unsanctioned services adopted by employees, outside the visibility of security and IT. The page uses the phrase for stage 01 with a qualifier: shadow IT in miniature, where the boundary was crossed not by a policy violation but by a browser feature doing exactly what it is designed to do.

Also from the csoh.org glossary entry Shadow IT: https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-shadow-it

== The support system held HAR files that routinely embed live session tokens
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: Collection
technique: T1213 | Data from Information Repositories
technique: T1539 | Steal Web Session Cookie
fact: Access | the compromised account could read the support case system
fact: HAR files | full browser network traces uploaded for troubleshooting
fact: Contents | every request and response header, live session tokens included
fact: Why it is asked for | the fastest way to diagnose a login or federation problem
Almost nobody treats a support attachment as a credential store.
notes:
Stage 02 is collection. The page maps it to T1213, Data from Information Repositories, and T1539, Steal Web Session Cookie.

The compromised service account could read Okta's customer support case management system, where customers upload HAR files. HAR stands for HTTP Archive. A HAR file is a complete recording of a browser's network activity: every request, every response, every header. Support engineers ask for them because they are the fastest way to diagnose a login or federation problem, and customers produce them willingly.

By construction, they also contain whatever was in those headers, which for an authenticated troubleshooting session means live session tokens. So the support system of an identity provider quietly became an aggregation point for working credentials belonging to its customers, with none of the protection those customers applied to credentials they knew they had.

Almost nobody on either side of a support ticket thinks of an attachment as a credential store, and the page calls that precisely the gap. The next slide explains session tokens in general.

== What a session token stands in for
layout: background
source: OWASP Session Management Cheat Sheet | https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Session_Management_Cheat_Sheet.html
OWASP says that once a session is authenticated, its session ID or token is temporarily equivalent to the strongest authentication method the application used.

Capturing that ID lets an attacker hijack the session and fully impersonate the user.
notes:
This is a background slide, from the OWASP Session Management Cheat Sheet, in general terms.

OWASP defines a web session as a sequence of HTTP request and response transactions associated with the same user. The session ID or token binds the user's authentication to that traffic. Once an authenticated session has been established, OWASP says, the session ID is temporarily equivalent to the strongest authentication method the application used, such as a username and password, one-time passwords or a smartcard.

OWASP adds that the disclosure, capture, prediction, brute force or fixation of a session ID leads to session hijacking, where an attacker is able to fully impersonate a victim user in the web application. For an attacker to reuse a session ID and hijack the session, OWASP notes, the session must still be active.

The page says the HAR files held live session tokens. Read this slide as the general idea only: the page does not describe how Okta's session tokens are built.

== Replayed tokens hijacked Okta sessions at five customers
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: Lateral Movement
technique: T1550.004 | Use Alternate Authentication Material: Web Session Cookie
fact: Technique | replay a stolen token; authentication has already happened
fact: Bypassed | password, MFA, and any sign-in-based detection
fact: Outcome | files of 134 customers accessed; 5 had sessions hijacked
fact: What limited it | token lifetime: only still-valid tokens were useful
The session simply continues, with no sign-in event that looks like an attack.
notes:
Stage 03 is where the tokens were used. The page files it under lateral movement and maps it to T1550.004, Use Alternate Authentication Material: Web Session Cookie.

A session token is proof that authentication already happened, so replaying one skips the entire login path. There is no password, no MFA prompt, no sign-in event that looks like an attack. MFA is multi-factor authentication. The session simply continues, and from the application's perspective the legitimate user is still working.

The page compares this with Storm-0558 and the Entra ID Actor token flaw: the same property made them so hard to detect. Here it arrived without any cryptography being broken. The tokens were simply lying in files people had uploaded voluntarily.

Files belonging to 134 customers were accessed, under 1% of Okta's base, and five had sessions hijacked. The page says that conversion rate reflects token lifetime more than anything else, since only tokens still valid when the attacker got to them were useful.

== A logging gap blinded Okta's investigation for fourteen days
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: Defense Impairment
technique: T1685.002 | Disable or Modify Tools: Disable or Modify Cloud Log
fact: The gap | case and Files tab access logged different event types
fact: Investigation | queried case access, so direct downloads did not appear
fact: Cost | 14 days in which the evidence supported "nothing happened"
fact: What broke it | a suspicious IP address from BeyondTrust on October 13
"We have logs" and "our queries would find this" are different claims.
notes:
The page calls this the most transferable detail in the whole incident, and says Okta deserves credit for publishing it. It maps the stage to T1685.002, Disable or Modify Tools: Disable or Modify Cloud Log. The page describes how Okta's support system logged file access; it does not say the attacker changed any log.

The support system logged file access differently depending on how a user reached the file. Opening an attachment from within a support case produced one event type, while navigating directly to the Files tab produced an entirely different one.

Okta's investigation queried case access, so the attacker's direct file downloads simply were not in the results. For fourteen days the reasonable conclusion from the available evidence was that nothing had happened. The gap closed only when BeyondTrust handed over a suspicious IP address on October 13 that could be searched for directly.

The general lesson, the page says, is that "we have logs" and "our queries would surface this behaviour" are different claims, and the difference is usually only discovered during an incident.

== One file, two paths, two event types
layout: compare
col: Opened from within a case | Produced one event type | The investigation queried case access
col: Opened from the Files tab | Produced an entirely different event type
The attacker's direct file downloads were not in the results, and for 14 days the evidence supported "nothing happened."
notes:
This slide draws the gap from the last slide.

The same file could be reached two ways in Okta's support system. Opening it from within a support case produced one event type. Navigating directly to the Files tab produced an entirely different one.

Okta's investigation queried case access, so the attacker's direct file downloads simply were not in the results. For 14 days the evidence supported the conclusion that nothing had happened, until BeyondTrust's IP address on October 13 gave the investigation something it could search for directly.

The page's defenses come back to this. Before an incident, take real questions, such as who accessed this file, and confirm your telemetry answers them regardless of which interface was used.

== Three customers found it before Okta did
layout: stage
stage: 05
phase: Detection
fact: September 29 | 1Password reports suspicious activity, the day after access began
fact: October 13 | BeyondTrust provides the attacker IP that unblocks Okta's investigation
fact: Independently | Cloudflare detects it and publishes its own account
fact: Write-ups | all three published detailed public accounts
In each case, the page says, a customer's monitoring of its own identity provider caught what the identity provider did not.
notes:
Stage 05 is detection. The page maps it to no ATT&CK technique, so the slide shows no chip.

The page says the sequence matters. 1Password saw something wrong the day after access began and told Okta: it reported suspicious activity on September 29. BeyondTrust detected the token replay against its own tenant, escalated, and eventually provided the IP address that unblocked the investigation, on October 13. Cloudflare detected it independently and published its own account.

In each case a customer's monitoring of its own identity provider caught what the identity provider did not. The page calls that an uncomfortable but actionable finding: your vendor's detection capability is not a substitute for your own, particularly for the vendor that sits at the centre of your access model.

All three companies also published detailed public write-ups, and the page says that transparency is why this incident is teachable at all. The next slide explains identity providers and tenants in general.

== What an identity provider is
layout: background
source: csoh.org glossary, IdP (Identity Provider) | https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-idp
credit: Also from the csoh.org glossary entry Tenant
An identity provider, or IdP, is the system that authenticates users; the glossary names Okta, Entra ID and Google Workspace.

A tenant is a logical boundary for one customer's data, identities and configuration in a shared platform.
notes:
This is a background slide. It explains two general ideas from the csoh.org glossary.

An identity provider is the system that authenticates users. The glossary's examples are Okta, Azure AD, now called Entra ID, and Google Workspace.

A tenant is a logical boundary for one customer's data, identities and configuration in a shared platform. Multi-tenant software as a service keeps many tenants isolated on the same infrastructure.

The page uses both words at stage 05. BeyondTrust detected the token replay against its own tenant, and the page says each customer's monitoring of its own identity provider caught what the identity provider did not. It describes the identity provider as the vendor that sits at the centre of your access model.

Also from the csoh.org glossary entry Tenant: https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-tenant

== Okta's fixes address four layers, not just the immediate hole
layout: stage
stage: 06
phase: Response
fact: Immediate | disabled the compromised service account
fact: Root cause | blocked personal Google profile sign-in via Chrome Enterprise policy on managed laptops
fact: Visibility | enhanced detection and monitoring for the support system
fact: Durable | session token binding to network location, shipped as a feature
The page calls binding the durable fix: HAR files will keep containing tokens no matter what anyone asks customers to do.
notes:
Stage 06 is Okta's response. The page maps it to no ATT&CK technique, so the slide shows no chip. The page says the remediation list is worth reading as a template, because it addresses four different layers rather than just the immediate hole.

The compromised service account was disabled. Personal Google profile sign-in was blocked via Chrome Enterprise policy on managed laptops, which the page says closes the root cause structurally rather than by asking people to be careful. Detection and monitoring for the support system were improved, addressing the logging gap.

And session token binding to network location was released as a product feature. The page says that attacks the underlying problem: a bound token cannot be replayed from the attacker's network even if it leaks. It calls that the durable fix, because HAR files will keep containing tokens no matter what anyone asks customers to do.

The next slide explains session binding in general.

== What binding a session to the client does
layout: background
source: OWASP Session Management Cheat Sheet | https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Session_Management_Cheat_Sheet.html
OWASP recommends binding a session ID to client properties, such as the client IP address. A change mid-session is a very good indicator of hijacking attempts.

OWASP warns a skilled attacker sharing the victim's network can bypass it.
notes:
This is a background slide, from the OWASP Session Management Cheat Sheet, in general terms.

To detect, and in some scenarios protect against, session hijacking, OWASP highly recommends binding the session ID to other user or client properties, such as the client IP address. If the application sees one of those properties change in the middle of an established session, OWASP calls that a very good indicator of session manipulation and hijacking attempts, which can be used to alert on or terminate the suspicious session.

OWASP also says these properties cannot be relied on alone to defend against session attacks. A skilled attacker can reuse the victim's IP address by sharing the same network, as is common on Wi-Fi hotspots, or by using the same outbound web proxy, as is common in corporate environments.

The page says Okta released session token binding to network location as a product feature. It does not describe how Okta's feature works, so read this slide as the general idea only.

== Six defenses against six stages
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04 | 05 | 06
row: Sanitize HAR files before upload | 02
row: Treat vendor support systems as attack surface | 02
row: Bind session tokens | 03 | 06
row: Block personal browser profiles by policy | 01 | 06
row: Monitor your IdP yourself | 03 | 05
row: Test what your logs can answer | 04
Every stage is tied to at least one defense. In stage 06, two defenses name fixes Okta itself made.
notes:
This matrix sets the page's six defenses against its six stages. A cell is marked only where a defense's own words name something from that stage.

Sanitizing HAR files and treating vendor support systems as attack surface both answer stage 02: browser traces sent to a vendor, and a support system that held live session tokens. Binding answers stage 03, where the page says the token replay only worked because a session token was portable. Blocking personal browser profiles answers stage 01's root cause, Chrome syncing a saved credential into a personal Google account. Monitoring your IdP yourself answers stage 05, three customers finding this before Okta did, and stage 03, sessions that appear without an authentication event. Testing your logs answers stage 04's two paths to one file.

Stage 06 is Okta's response. The page says Okta shipped exactly the binding feature in response, and both stage 06 and the profile defense name Chrome Enterprise policy.

Two near misses are not marked. Sanitizing also says to invalidate the captured session, and the vendor defense asks how access is logged, but neither names stage 03's replay or stage 04's two event types.

== Sanitize HAR files; treat vendor support as attack surface
layout: compare
col: Sanitize HAR files: stage 02 | Strip the tokens before you upload a browser trace | Then invalidate the session you captured it from | Handle any file with full request headers as a credential dump
col: Vendor support systems: stage 02 | Ask what support artifacts vendors keep, and for how long | Ask who can read them, and how access is logged | Then minimize what you send
The support ticket is a channel through which your credentials leave your control, the page says, and almost nobody governs it.
notes:
Two defenses come from stage 02, the support system full of other people's session tokens.

First, sanitize HAR files before you upload them, and assume nobody else will. If your vendor asks for a browser trace, strip the tokens first. There are open-source HAR sanitizers, and the browser's own export includes everything by default. Then invalidate the session you captured it from. The page says to treat any file containing full request headers as a credential dump for handling purposes, because that is exactly what it is.

Second, treat vendor support systems as part of your attack surface. The page says this one held live session tokens for hundreds of customers. Ask vendors what support artifacts they retain, for how long, who can read them, and how access is logged, then minimize what you send.

The page's reason: the support ticket is a channel through which your credentials leave your control, and almost nobody governs it.

== Bind session tokens; block personal profiles by policy
layout: compare
col: Bind tokens: stages 03, 06 | Replay only worked because a session token was portable | Bind to network location or device where your IdP offers it | Shorten session lifetimes for privileged roles
col: Personal profiles: stages 01, 06 | Restrict browser sign-in to your organization's accounts | Disable the browser password manager for corporate credentials | Require an enterprise password manager
The failure was a default behaviour, the page says, so the fix has to be a configuration rather than a request.
notes:
Two defenses whose fixes also appear in stage 06, Okta's response.

Bind session tokens so a stolen one is useless elsewhere. The page says the token replay only worked because a session token was portable. Where your identity provider offers it, enable binding to network location or device, and shorten session lifetimes for privileged roles so leaked tokens expire before they can be used. Okta shipped exactly this feature in response, and the page says the general principle applies to every session-issuing system you run.

Block personal browser profiles on managed devices with policy, not with training. The root cause was Chrome syncing a saved credential into a personal Google account. Chrome Enterprise policy can restrict sign-in to your organization's accounts, and equivalent controls exist for other browsers. Stage 06 says Okta blocked personal Google profile sign-in with Chrome Enterprise policy on managed laptops.

Pair it with disabling the browser password manager for corporate credentials entirely and requiring an enterprise password manager. The page says the failure here was a default behaviour, so the fix has to be a configuration rather than a request.

== Monitor your IdP yourself; test what your logs can answer
layout: compare
col: Monitor your IdP: stages 03, 05 | Ingest its system logs into your own SIEM | Alert on admin actions, impossible travel, privileged sessions from unfamiliar addresses | Alert on sessions with no corresponding authentication event
col: Test your logs: stage 04 | Take real questions: who accessed this file, read this record, exported this data | Confirm your telemetry answers them whichever interface was used | Run that as a tabletop against your own logs
Three customers found this before Okta did: your vendor's detection is not yours.
notes:
Two defenses about detection.

Monitor your identity provider yourself, because your vendor's detection is not yours. Three customers found this before Okta did. Ingest your IdP's system logs into your own SIEM, a security information and event management system. Alert on administrative actions, impossible-travel sessions, and privileged sessions from unfamiliar addresses. And specifically alert on sessions that appear without a corresponding authentication event, which the page calls the signature of exactly this attack. Stage 03 says why: a replayed token skips the entire login path, with no sign-in event that looks like an attack.

Test whether your logs answer the questions an investigation will ask. The fourteen-day gap came from two paths to the same file generating different event types. Before an incident, take real questions, such as who accessed this file, who read this record and who exported this data, and confirm your telemetry answers them regardless of which interface was used. Run that as a tabletop against your own logs. The page says the gaps are rarely where you expect.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: Do you strip tokens from HAR files before sending them, then invalidate that session?
check: What support artifacts does each vendor keep, how long, and who reads them?
check: Are sessions bound to network location or device, where your IdP offers it?
check: Can staff sign managed browsers into personal accounts?
check: Do your IdP's system logs reach your own SIEM?
check: Would you alert on a session with no corresponding authentication event?
check: Could your logs say who opened a file, whichever interface was used?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

Ask whether anyone strips tokens from a HAR file before it goes to a vendor, and invalidates the session it was captured from. Ask what support artifacts each vendor retains, for how long, who can read them, and how access is logged.

Ask whether your sessions are bound to network location or device where your identity provider offers it, and whether privileged roles have short session lifetimes. Ask whether staff can sign a managed browser into a personal account, and whether corporate credentials can be saved in the browser's own password manager.

Ask whether your IdP's system logs reach your own SIEM, and whether anything would alert on a session that appears without a corresponding authentication event. Finally, ask whether your logs could say who opened a file regardless of which interface was used.

== Would your queries find it?
layout: question
Okta had logs, but its investigation queried case access, and the attacker's direct file downloads were not in the results.

Pick one question an investigation would ask. Would your logs answer it, whichever interface was used?
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one before moving on.

Okta's support system logged a file opened from within a case as one event type, and the same file opened from the Files tab as an entirely different one. The investigation queried case access, so for fourteen days the evidence supported the conclusion that nothing had happened.

Ask each person to name one specific question, such as who accessed this file, and one interface in their own environment that might log it differently. The answer slide gives what the page says to do about it.

== Test the questions before an incident
layout: answer
Confirm your telemetry answers real questions whichever interface was used, the page says.
- Who accessed this file, read this record, exported this data?
- Run it as a tabletop against your own logs; the gaps are rarely where you expect.
notes:
The page's answer is to test your logs against the questions an investigation will ask, before an incident.

Take real questions: who accessed this file, who read this record, who exported this data. Confirm your telemetry answers them regardless of which interface was used. Run that as a tabletop against your own logs, because the page says the gaps are rarely where you expect.

The page's general lesson sits behind this: "we have logs" and "our queries would surface this behaviour" are different claims, and the difference is usually only discovered during an incident.

Bring the group's examples back to this. For each interface named, ask whether anyone has checked that it logs the same event.

== Who would notice first if your IdP were breached?
layout: question
In each case here, a customer's monitoring of its own identity provider caught what the identity provider did not.

What in your monitoring would flag a session that appeared without a corresponding authentication event?
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one too.

Three customers found this before Okta did. 1Password reported suspicious activity the day after access began, BeyondTrust detected the token replay against its own tenant, and Cloudflare detected it independently. A replayed session token produces no password, no MFA prompt and no sign-in event that looks like an attack.

Ask each person what in their own monitoring would notice a session that appears without a corresponding authentication event, and whose logs it would come from. The answer slide gives what the page recommends.

== Monitor your identity provider yourself
layout: answer
Your vendor's detection is not yours, the page says.
- Ingest your IdP's system logs into your own SIEM.
- Alert on sessions that appear without a corresponding authentication event.
notes:
The page's answer is to monitor your identity provider yourself, because your vendor's detection is not a substitute for your own, particularly for the vendor that sits at the centre of your access model.

Ingest your IdP's system logs into your own SIEM. Alert on administrative actions, impossible-travel sessions, and privileged sessions from unfamiliar addresses. And specifically alert on sessions that appear without a corresponding authentication event, which the page calls the signature of exactly this attack.

Bring the group's answers back to this. If their monitoring relies on the provider's own detection, this incident is the page's example of what that can miss.

